System Design & Microservices Interview Notes
1. Single Server Architecture
Explanation

Single server architecture is a setup where frontend, backend, and database run on the same server. Client requests first go to DNS, which converts domain name into IP address, then request reaches server for processing. Backend processes business logic, communicates with database, and sends response back to client. This architecture is simple and suitable for small applications and initial startup projects.

Suitable Cases
Small applications
College projects
Internal tools
MVP products
Advantages
Easy setup
Low cost
Simple deployment
Disadvantages
Single point of failure
Limited scalability
Performance bottleneck
2. DNS (Domain Name System)
Explanation

DNS converts human-readable domain names like:

google.com

into IP addresses so browsers can locate servers on internet. Without DNS, users would need to remember server IP addresses directly. DNS acts like internet phonebook.

Suitable Cases

Used in all internet applications and websites.

3. HTTP Server & Tomcat
HTTP Server
Explanation

HTTP server receives and processes HTTP/HTTPS requests from clients. It handles static content, SSL, routing, and load balancing. Examples include Nginx and Apache HTTP Server.

Suitable Cases
Serving static files
Reverse proxy
Load balancing
Web hosting
Tomcat
Explanation

Apache Tomcat is a Java servlet container used to run Java applications like Spring Boot and JSP applications. It converts HTTP requests into Java objects and executes backend business logic.

Suitable Cases
Java web applications
Spring Boot applications
Enterprise backend systems
4. Relational Database (SQL)
Explanation

Relational databases store data in tables with rows and columns and maintain relationships using primary and foreign keys. They use SQL language and support ACID transactions for strong consistency.

Examples
MySQL
PostgreSQL
Oracle Database
Suitable Cases
Banking systems
ERP applications
Payment systems
Inventory management
Advantages
Strong consistency
ACID transactions
Complex joins supported
Disadvantages
Hard horizontal scaling
Fixed schema
Less flexible
5. Non-Relational Database (NoSQL)
Explanation

NoSQL databases store flexible data formats like JSON, key-value, graph, or documents. They support high scalability and distributed architectures.

Examples
MongoDB
Redis
Cassandra
Suitable Cases
Social media
Real-time chat
Big data systems
Caching
Advantages
Horizontal scalability
Flexible schema
High performance
Disadvantages
Weak relationships
Limited transaction support
6. Vertical Scaling
Explanation

Vertical scaling means increasing server resources such as CPU, RAM, or storage within same server. It is simple but limited by hardware capacity.

Suitable Cases
Small systems
Initial startup applications
Monolithic applications
Advantages
Easy implementation
Simple maintenance
Disadvantages
Single point of failure
Hardware limits
7. Horizontal Scaling
Explanation

Horizontal scaling means adding multiple servers and distributing traffic using load balancers. It improves scalability and fault tolerance.

Suitable Cases
Large-scale applications
Distributed systems
Cloud-native applications
Advantages
High scalability
Fault tolerance
Better availability
Disadvantages
Complex architecture
Distributed system challenges
8. Load Balancer
Explanation

Load balancer distributes incoming traffic across multiple servers to improve performance, availability, and scalability.

Round Robin
Explanation

Requests are distributed equally among servers sequentially.

Suitable Cases
Equal-capacity servers
Simple applications
IP Hashing
Explanation

Uses client IP address to route requests to same server consistently.

Suitable Cases
Session-based applications
Shopping cart systems
Least Connection
Explanation

Traffic goes to server with least active connections.

Suitable Cases
Chat systems
Streaming applications
Least Response Time
Explanation

Requests routed to fastest responding server.

Suitable Cases
Low-latency systems
Financial applications
Health Check
Explanation

Load balancer continuously checks server health and removes failed servers.

Suitable Cases
High availability systems
Production environments
Weighted Round Robin
Explanation

Traffic distributed based on server weight/capacity.

Suitable Cases
Unequal hardware environments
Geographical Load Balancing
Explanation

Traffic routed based on user location.

Suitable Cases
Global applications
CDN systems
9. OSI Model
Explanation

OSI model is a 7-layer networking framework explaining how data travels across network systems.

Layers
Physical Layer
Data Link Layer
Network Layer
Transport Layer
Session Layer
Presentation Layer
Application Layer
Important Points
Router works at Layer 3
Switch works at Layer 2
HTTP works at Application Layer
TCP/UDP work at Transport Layer
