Network latency
Hard debugging
20. API Gateway Pattern
Explanation

API Gateway provides single entry point for all client requests and handles routing, authentication, monitoring, and load balancing.

Suitable Cases
Microservices systems
Cloud-native applications
Advantages
Centralized security
Simplified frontend communication
Disadvantages
Single point of failure
21. Database per Service Pattern
Explanation

Each microservice owns its own database independently.

Suitable Cases
Independent scalable services
Polyglot persistence systems
22. Circuit Breaker Pattern
Explanation

Circuit breaker prevents repeated calls to failing services and avoids cascading failures.

Suitable Cases
External APIs
Distributed systems
23. Service Discovery Pattern
Explanation

Services dynamically register and discover each other without hardcoded IP addresses.

Suitable Cases
Kubernetes environments
Dynamic cloud systems
24. Event Sourcing Pattern
Explanation

All state changes stored as immutable events instead of updating current values directly.

Suitable Cases
Audit systems
Financial systems
25. CQRS Pattern
Explanation

Separates read operations and write operations into different models.

Suitable Cases
High-read applications
Reporting systems
26. Saga Pattern
Explanation

Saga pattern manages distributed transactions using local transactions and compensating rollback actions.

Suitable Cases
E-commerce order systems
Banking workflows
27. Strangler Fig Pattern
Explanation

Gradually replaces monolithic applications with microservices step by step.

Suitable Cases
Legacy system migration
28. Bulkhead Pattern
Explanation

Isolates resources so failure in one component does not affect others.

Suitable Cases
High availability systems
29. Sidecar Pattern
Explanation

Deploys helper components alongside application containers for logging, monitoring, or networking.

Suitable Cases
Kubernetes environments
Service mesh architectures
30. Data Consistency in Microservices
Explanation

Microservices maintain consistency using eventual consistency and Saga pattern instead of distributed ACID transactions.

Techniques
Saga Pattern
Event-Driven Architecture
Outbox Pattern
Kafka/RabbitMQ communication
Suitable Cases
Distributed systems
Event-driven microservices
